 its least vertex, neighbours in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"incidentEdges","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"case"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"connectedSequentialBreadthFirst"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/connectedsequentialbreadthfirst","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialBreadthFirst","title":"ColoringStrategy.connectedSequentialBreadthFirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/coloredNeighbors":{"abstract":[{"text":"First the vertex of greatest degree (the least index on ties), then repeatedly the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"uncoloured vertex with the most coloured neighbours. Ties among those fall as a two-way","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"indexed binary max-heap breaks them, not by index: vertices are pushed in index order and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"raised neighbour by neighbour in ascending order; an entry moves above an equal parent and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"sinks to the left child unless the right one is strictly greater. O((n + m) log n).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"coloredNeighbors","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/coloredneighbors","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/coloredNeighbors","title":"ColoringStrategy.coloredNeighbors"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy":{"title":"ColoringStrategy","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"enum","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"ColoringStrategy","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy","abstract":[{"text":"How ","type":"text"},{"code":"greedyColoring(strategy:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" orders the vertices. Each vertex in turn takes the least colour","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"none of its already coloured neighbours has (first fit). Degrees are simple degrees (distinct","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"other neighbours, self-loops left out); ties go to the lesser vertex index, except for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":".coloredNeighbors","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"ColoringStrategy","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/saturationLargestFirst":{"abstract":[{"text":"DSatur (Brélaz 1979): first a vertex of greatest degree, then repeatedly the uncoloured","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex with the most distinct colours among its neighbours, ties to the greatest degree in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the whole graph, then to the lesser index. Exact on bipartite graphs. O((n + m) log n).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"saturationLargestFirst","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/saturationLargestFirst","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/saturationlargestfirst","title":"ColoringStrategy.saturationLargestFirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/smallestLast":{"abstract":[{"text":"Matula–Beck: repeatedly remove a vertex of least degree in what is left (the least index on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ties), then colour in the reverse of that removal order. At most degeneracy + 1 colours, so","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"at most 6 on a planar graph. O((n + m) log n).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"smallestLast","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/smallestlast","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/smallestLast","title":"ColoringStrategy.smallestLast"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/independentSet":{"title":"ColoringStrategy.independentSet","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"independentSet","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/independentset","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Colour classes one at a time: class k is a maximal independent set of the vertices not yet"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"coloured, built by repeatedly taking the one with the fewest neighbours among those still"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"available (the least index on ties) and making it and its neighbours unavailable."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"O(χ (n + m) log n) for χ classes."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/independentSet"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/Equatable-Implementations":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","abstract":[],"role":"collectionGroup","url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/equatable-implementations","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/Equatable-Implementations","title":"Equatable Implementations"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/connectedSequentialDepthFirst":{"title":"ColoringStrategy.connectedSequentialDepthFirst","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"connectedSequentialDepthFirst","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/connectedsequentialdepthfirst","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The same in depth-first preorder. O(n + m)."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/connectedSequentialDepthFirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/documentation/ColoringModule/ColoringStrategy/largestFirst":{"title":"ColoringStrategy.largestFirst","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"case","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"largestFirst","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/coloringmodule\/coloringstrategy\/largestfirst","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Degree descending, the lesser index first on ties: Welsh–Powell, which colours class by"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"class in this order and gives the same colouring as first fit. O(n + m)."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/documentation\/ColoringModule\/ColoringStrategy\/largestFirst"},"doc://ColoringModule/s16SendableMetatypeP":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/ColoringModule\/s16SendableMetatypeP","title":"Swift.SendableMetatype","type":"unresolvable"}}}